Keep it kid-friendly but not condescending. Use short paragraphs. Maybe under 300 words.

Imagine you have a giant pile of medicine books and a robot that must answer questions about them.

**1. Cut the books into cards.**
The books were too big, so I cut them into small cards (called "chunks"). Each card says one useful thing, like "these two medicines don't mix well."

**2. Give every card a "meaning fingerprint."**
A computer read each card and turned it into a long list of numbers — like a fingerprint of what the card *means*. Cards about similar ideas get similar fingerprints. That's how the computer finds things by meaning, not just by exact words.

**3. Hire two librarians.**
- **Word librarian (BM25):** finds cards that contain the exact words you typed. Fast, but dumb about synonyms.
- **Meaning librarian (semantic search):** finds cards that *mean* the same thing, even with different words.
- **Team librarian (hybrid):** asks both and combines their answers.

**4. Test them.**
I wrote a little quiz with real questions, like "Can an older person on Amlodipine also take Ibuprofen?" and checked whether each librarian found the right cards.

**What I learned:**
- The **meaning librarian** was best at questions with different wording (like "dosage" vs "dosages").
- The **word librarian** was great when the exact rare medicine names appeared.
- The **team librarian** did best on the big real question — it found the right interaction cards that the others missed.
- Some questions failed because the question says "aspirin" but the books say "acetylsalicylic acid." Same medicine, different name. We'll fix that next week.

**Bonus:** I also fixed a sorting machine from last week that was gluing cards together by mistake, and I grouped duplicate medicine cards so they don't drown out the good answers.

Then I wrote it all down in a report.

