Title: Sharing is discontinued - govern what leaves | agents.cli
URL: https://www.agentscli.com/course/opencode/share-and-headless/share-links/
Published: N/A
Author: N/A
Highlights:
Until October 2026 OpenCode did exactly that: `/share` synced the conversation to OpenCode’s servers and minted a public `opncd.ai/s/ ` link. That feature has been discontinued. The v1.19.1 release notes (2026-10-15) say: “Session sharing has been discontinued. Existing share links and session-sharing commands are no longer available, and importing sessions from share URLs is unsupported” (release v1.19.1, as of October 2026). The source agrees. In v1.19.4 the share service fails with `Session sharing has been discontinued`, `unshare` only clears the local link, and `opencode run --share` is a hidden deprecated flag that prints the same message (`share/session.ts`, `run.ts`). The GitHub integration no longer attaches a session link to its comments either.

The public sharing documentation page has been removed, and the `/share` entry and `share` config row are gone from the TUI and config pages (as of October 25, 2026). Whether a pre-1.19.1 client can still publish a link depends on the share service, which this course did not test. Treat sharing as gone.

OpenCode 2 has no sharing either: its `--help` lists no share command and the v2 docs have no sharing page. Session export moved under `session`: use `opencode session export [session]`, which also takes `--sanitize` to redact sensitive transcript and file data, in place of `opencode export` (checked with `opencode session export --help` on v2.1.2, October 2026). Review a sanitized export before you send it anyway.

## Get a second opinion without a share link

The need did not go away; the transport did. Three substitutes, in order of how much leaves your machine:

- Describe the bug, with evidence. The coworker needs the failing input, the observed and expected output, and the two fixes you tried. For the Atom case that is the feed entry with its offset, the stored time, and the dedupe key it produced. Paste that into chat. It is smaller than a transcript and easier to answer.
- Export the session to a file.`opencode export [sessionID]` writes the session as JSON, and the TUI has a `session_export` keybind that opens it in your editor (CLI documentation, `keybind.ts`, as of October 2026). You choose who receives the file. A JSON export is raw (prompts, tool output, file contents the agent read), so review it before sending it anywhere.
- Reproduce in a clean directory. A minimal repro of the parser bug with the internal feed URLs removed can go to anyone, and the person helping can run it themselves.

## What still leaves the machine

Ending the share feature does not make a session local-only. Every turn sends your prompts, the files the agent read, and tool output to whichever model provider answers it. That traffic is governed by your provider choice and your permission map, not by anything sharing-related. For `feedmill`, the controls that matter are the ones from earlier chapters: a local model for private feeds (local and custom models) and `webfetch`/`websearch` gated in the permission map.

The `share` key (`manual`, `auto`, `disabled`) is still accepted by the v1.19.4 config schema (`config.ts`), but with sharing removed the setting no longer controls any upload. If you committed `"share": "disabled"` to a repo’s `opencode.json` as a guard, it is harmless; it is no longer doing a job, and it is not a substitute for the controls above.

---

Title: OpenCode - Managing and Searching Conversations | Midimaze
URL: https://midimaze.com/articles/3fr0ve010v9
Published: 2026-01-30T12:01:53.000Z
Author: N/A
Highlights:
### Method 4: Export and Search (Advanced)

Export a specific session:

```
opencode export <sessionID>

```

This outputs the session as JSON, which you can:

- Pipe to`jq` for JSON parsing
- Grep for specific content
- Archive for documentation

Export current session (from within OpenCode TUI):

```
/export

```

This opens your default editor (`$EDITOR`) with the conversation in Markdown format.

...

- Creating documentation from conversations
- Searching conversation content with external tools
- Archiving important sessions

...

### Session Persistence

- Sessions are stored locally on your machine
- Each working directory can have multiple sessions
- Sessions persist across OpenCode restarts
- Shared sessions (via`/share`) get a public URL but remain private by default

...

### 2. Combine with /share for Collaboration

```
/share

```

Copy the URL, share with teammates, then use`--session ` to resume the exact conversation later.

...

# From within OpenCode TUI
/export

...

```

...

If you have an exported markdown file:

Unfortunately, OpenCode doesn't have a built-in "import" feature to recreate sessions from markdown exports. However, you can:

1. Start a new OpenCode session
2. Reference the exported markdown file
3. Ask OpenCode to continue from that context

```
opencode run --title "Restored: [Original Title]" "Continue from this exported session: @path/to/exported-session.md"

```

...

## Related Commands

- `/new`- Start fresh session (current one saved automatically)
- `/export`- Export current session to Markdown
- `/share`- Share session publicly
- `/compact`- Summarize long sessions
- `/undo`- Revert last message in current session

...

### "Share conversation with teammate, then resume later"

1. In conversation:`/share`
2. Copy URL, send to teammate
3. Later:`opencode --session `(extract from URL)

